"To find the distance from the origin to the point \\((-8,6)\\), we use the distance formula. The distance formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nIn this case, the origin is \\((0,0)\\) and the point is \\((-8,6)\\). Plugging these coordinates into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{((-8) - 0)^2 + (6 - 0)^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(-8)^2 + 6^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{64 + 36}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{100}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance from the origin to the point \\((-8,6)\\) is \\(10\\) units.\n\n\\(\\boxed{10}\\)"
"To find \\( f(-10) \\), we need to determine which piece of the piecewise function to use. The function \\( f(x) \\) is defined as:\n\n\\[\nf(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n3x + 5 & \\text{if } x < -3, \\\\\n7 - 4x & \\text{if } x \\ge -3.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( -10 \\) is less than \\( -3 \\), we use the first piece of the function:\n\n\\[\nf(-10) = 3(-10) + 5\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the calculation:\n\n\\[\nf(-10) = 3(-10) + 5 = -30 + 5 = -25\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f(-10) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-25}\\)."
