"First, let's determine the total amount of supplies purchased. If twelve people purchased supplies for a ten-day camping trip, the total amount of supplies is:\n\n\\[ 12 \\text{ people} \\times 10 \\text{ days} = 120 \\text{ person-days} \\]\n\nNow, three more people join, making the total number of people:\n\n\\[ 12 \\text{ people} + 3 \\text{ people} = 15 \\text{ people} \\]\n\nWe need to find out how many days the supplies will last for 15 people. Since the total amount of supplies is 120 person-days, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[ 15 \\text{ people} \\times d \\text{ days} = 120 \\text{ person-days} \\]\n\nSolving for \\( d \\):\n\n\\[ d = \\frac{120 \\text{ person-days}}{15 \\text{ people}} = 8 \\text{ days} \\]\n\nTherefore, the supplies will last for \\(\\boxed{8}\\) days if the original daily share for each person is not changed."
"To solve the equation \\((3^k)^6 = 3^6\\), we can use the properties of exponents. Specifically, we will use the property \\((a^m)^n = a^{mn}\\).\n\nStep 1: Apply the exponent property to the left side of the equation.\n\\[\n(3^k)^6 = 3^{6k}\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Rewrite the equation with the simplified left side.\n\\[\n3^{6k} = 3^6\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Since the bases are the same, set the exponents equal to each other.\n\\[\n6k = 6\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Solve for \\(k\\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 6.\n\\[\nk = \\frac{6}{6} = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(k\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."
"First, we need to find the polynomial \\( g(x) \\). Since \\( g(x) = f(x + 1) \\), we substitute \\( x + 1 \\) into the polynomial \\( f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\ng(x) = f(x + 1) = (x + 1)^7 - 3(x + 1)^3 + 2\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the sum of the coefficients of \\( g(x) \\). The sum of the coefficients of a polynomial is obtained by evaluating the polynomial at \\( x = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\ng(1) = (1 + 1)^7 - 3(1 + 1)^3 + 2\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[\ng(1) = 2^7 - 3 \\cdot 2^3 + 2\n\\]\n\nCalculate the powers:\n\n\\[\ng(1) = 128 - 24 + 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\ng(1) = 106\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the coefficients of \\( g(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{106}\\)."
"Given the ratio of \\( x \\) to \\( y \\) is \\( 1 \\) to \\( 2 \\), we can express this relationship as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{y} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\ny = 2x\n\\]\n\nWe are also given the equation \\( y = 4x - 36 \\). Substituting \\( y = 2x \\) into this equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n2x = 4x - 36\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we first isolate \\( x \\) on one side of the equation. Subtract \\( 2x \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n2x - 2x = 4x - 2x - 36\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n0 = 2x - 36\n\\]\n\nAdd 36 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n36 = 2x\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\nx = 18\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{18}\\)."
"To find the numerical value of the expression \\((b-c)^2 + a(b+c)\\) given \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 3\\), and \\(c = 4\\), we will substitute these values into the expression and simplify step by step.\n\nFirst, calculate \\(b - c\\):\n\\[\nb - c = 3 - 4 = -1\n\\]\n\nNext, square the result:\n\\[\n(b - c)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate \\(b + c\\):\n\\[\nb + c = 3 + 4 = 7\n\\]\n\nThen, calculate \\(a(b + c)\\):\n\\[\na(b + c) = 2 \\cdot 7 = 14\n\\]\n\nFinally, add the two results together:\n\\[\n(b - c)^2 + a(b + c) = 1 + 14 = 15\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the numerical value of the expression is \\(\\boxed{15}\\)."
