"To find the value of \\( f(g(2)) \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Evaluate \\( g(2) \\).\n2. Use the result from step 1 to evaluate \\( f(g(2)) \\).\n\n**Step 1: Evaluate \\( g(2) \\).**\n\nThe function \\( g(x) \\) is given by \\( g(x) = x^2 + 3 \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the function \\( g(x) \\):\n\\[ g(2) = 2^2 + 3 = 4 + 3 = 7. \\]\n\n**Step 2: Evaluate \\( f(g(2)) \\).**\n\nThe function \\( f(x) \\) is given by \\( f(x) = x + 1 \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( x = g(2) = 7 \\) into the function \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ f(g(2)) = f(7) = 7 + 1 = 8. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f(g(2)) \\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).\n\n\\boxed{8}"
