"For the quadratic equation \\(3x^2 + 7x + c = 0\\) to have two real roots, the discriminant must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\).\n\nHere, \\(a = 3\\), \\(b = 7\\), and \\(c = c\\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\\[\n\\Delta = 7^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot c = 49 - 12c\n\\]\n\nFor the discriminant to be non-negative, we need:\n\\[\n49 - 12c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\\[\n49 \\geq 12c\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{4} \\geq c\n\\]\n\nThe positive integer values of \\(c\\) that satisfy this inequality are \\(c = 1, 2, 3, 4\\).\n\nThe product of these values is:\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 24\n\\]\n\nSolution: 24"
