"To determine how many miles William ran, we need to use the formula for distance, which is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} \\]\n\nFirst, we need to convert the time from seconds to hours because the speed is given in miles per hour. There are 3600 seconds in an hour, so we convert 75 seconds to hours:\n\n\\[ 75 \\text{ seconds} = \\frac{75}{3600} \\text{ hours} = \\frac{1}{48} \\text{ hours} \\]\n\nNext, we use the speed formula to find the distance:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = 8 \\text{ miles per hour} \\times \\frac{1}{48} \\text{ hours} \\]\n\nTo multiply these values, we can simplify the calculation:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = 8 \\times \\frac{1}{48} = \\frac{8}{48} = \\frac{1}{6} \\]\n\nTherefore, the distance William ran is \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\) miles."
"Let the two integers be \\( a \\) and \\( b \\). According to the problem, we have the equation:\n\n\\[ ab - a - b = 39 \\]\n\nWe can rewrite this equation as:\n\n\\[ (a-1)(b-1) = 40 \\]\n\nSince \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are relatively prime and each is less than 20, we need to find pairs of factors of 40 that are relatively prime. The pairs of factors of 40 are:\n\n\\[ (1, 40), (2, 20), (4, 10), (5, 8) \\]\n\nAmong these pairs, only the pair \\((5, 8)\\) is relatively prime. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[ a-1 = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b-1 = 8 \\]\n\nThus, \\( a = 6 \\) and \\( b = 9 \\). The sum of the two integers is:\n\n\\[ a + b = 6 + 9 = 15 \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{15}\\)."
