"The quadratic inequality $x^2 + bx + c > 0$ holds for $x \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (3, \\infty)$. This means that the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2 + bx + c = 0$ are $x = -2$ and $x = 3$. Therefore, we can write the quadratic equation as:\n\n\\[x^2 + bx + c = (x + 2)(x - 3).\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[(x + 2)(x - 3) = x^2 - x - 6.\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[x^2 + bx + c = x^2 - x - 6.\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients, we see that:\n\n\\[b = -1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad c = -6.\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $b + c$ is:\n\n\\[b + c = -1 + (-6) = -7.\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{-7}\\).\nI hope it is correct."
