"To determine the largest number \\(c\\) such that the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 5x + c = 0\\) has at least one real solution, we need to ensure that the discriminant of the quadratic equation is non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 5x + c = 0\\), we have \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 5\\), and \\(c = c\\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 5^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot c = 25 - 8c\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have at least one real solution, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[\n25 - 8c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving this inequality:\n\n\\[\n25 - 8c \\geq 0 \\implies 25 \\geq 8c \\implies c = \\boxed{\\frac{25}{8}}\n\\]"
"We start with the given system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nx + y - z &= -8, \\\\\nx - y + z &= 18, \\\\\n-x + y + z &= 30.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we add all three equations together:\n\\[\n(x + y - z) + (x - y + z) + (-x + y + z) = -8 + 18 + 30.\n\\]\nSimplifying the left-hand side, we get:\n\\[\n2x + 2y + 2z = 40.\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 2, we find:\n\\[\nx + y + z = 20.\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the second equation from this result:\n\\[\n(x + y + z) - (x - y + z) = 20 - 18.\n\\]\nSimplifying the left-hand side, we get:\n\\[\n2y = 2.\n\\]\nSolving for \\( y \\), we find:\n\\[\ny = 1 \\Rightarrow y = \\boxed{1}.\n\\]"
