"To find the slope of a line perpendicular to the line given by the equation \\(\\frac{x}{4} - \\frac{y}{5} = 1\\), we first need to determine the slope of the given line.\n\nThe given equation is in the form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope. Let's rewrite the given equation in this form:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{4} - \\frac{y}{5} = 1\n\\]\n\nMultiply every term by 20 to clear the denominators:\n\n\\[\n5x - 4y = 20\n\\]\n\nRearrange to solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{5}{4}x - 5\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(m\\) of the given line is \\(\\frac{5}{4}\\).\n\nThe slope of a line perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the original slope. Therefore, the slope of the perpendicular line is:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{1}{\\frac{5}{4}} = -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{4}{5}}\\)."
