"To find the vertical asymptotes of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2x}{x^2 - 5x - 14} \\), we need to determine the values of \\( x \\) that make the denominator zero.\n\nThe denominator is \\( x^2 - 5x - 14 \\). We solve this quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 5x - 14 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic equation:\n\\[\n(x - 7)(x + 2) = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions are \\( x = 7 \\) and \\( x = -2 \\). These are the vertical asymptotes.\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we compare the degrees of the polynomial in the numerator and the denominator. Both the numerator and the denominator are quadratic polynomials (degree 2). Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is given by the ratio of the leading coefficients:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2}{1} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we sum the values:\n\\[\na + b + c = 7 + (-2) + 2 = \\boxed{7}\n\\]"
"To find the current \\( I \\) in the equation \\( V = IZ \\), we need to divide the voltage \\( V \\) by the impedance \\( Z \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ V = 1 + i \\]\n\\[ Z = 2 - i \\]\n\nWe perform the division:\n\\[ I = \\frac{V}{Z} = \\frac{1 + i}{2 - i} \\]\n\nTo simplify this, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is \\( 2 + i \\):\n\\[ I = \\frac{(1 + i)(2 + i)}{(2 - i)(2 + i)} \\]\n\\[ I = \\frac{2 + i + 2i + i^2}{4 - i^2} \\]\n\\[ I = \\frac{2 + 3i - 1}{4 + 1} \\]\n\\[ I = \\frac{1 + 3i}{5} \\]\n\nThus, the current \\( I \\) is:\n\\[ I = \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{3}{5}i \\]\nFinal Answer: I hope it is correct."
