"To find the least value of \\( b \\) such that \\( b^2 + 2b - 15 \\le 0 \\), we first solve the quadratic equation \\( b^2 + 2b - 15 = 0 \\).\n\nWe can factor the quadratic equation:\n\\[ b^2 + 2b - 15 = (b + 5)(b - 3) = 0. \\]\n\nThis gives us the roots:\n\\[ b = -5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 3. \\]\n\nThe quadratic inequality \\( b^2 + 2b - 15 \\le 0 \\) holds between the roots \\( b = -5 \\) and \\( b = 3 \\). Therefore, the solution to the inequality is:\n\\[ -5 \\le b \\le 3. \\]\n\nThe least value of \\( b \\) in this interval is \\( b = -5 \\).\n\nThus, the least value of \\( b \\) such that \\( b^2 + 2b - 15 \\le 0 \\) is \\(\\boxed{-5}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: \n\\(\\boxed{-5}\\)"
