"Let \\( x \\) be the smallest number which is one less than twice its reciprocal. We can express this relationship mathematically as:\n\n\\[ x = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{x} - 1 \\right) \\]\n\nFirst, simplify the equation:\n\n\\[ x = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{x} - 1 \\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{2}{x} - 2 \\]\n\nNext, eliminate the fraction by multiplying both sides by \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x^2 = 2 - 2x \\]\n\nRearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 2x - 2 = 0 \\]\n\nFactor the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ (x + 1)^2 = 0 \\]\n\nSolve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x + 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = -1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest number which is one less than twice its reciprocal is \\( \\boxed{-1} \\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(-1\\). I hope it is correct."
