"To determine the number of ways to select 5 students from a group of 6 students, we use the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of students, and \\( r \\) is the number of students to be selected.\n\nHere, \\( n = 6 \\) and \\( r = 5 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{5} = \\frac{6!}{5!(6-5)!} = \\frac{6!}{5! \\cdot 1!}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 1! = 1 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{5} = \\frac{6!}{5!}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\( 6! = 720 \\) and \\( 5! = 120 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{5} = \\frac{720}{120} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to select 5 students from a group of 6 students is \\( \\boxed{6}\\)."
