"To find the area of the circle circumscribed about an equilateral triangle with side length \\(6\\) units, we first need to determine the radius of the circumscribed circle.\n\nFor an equilateral triangle with side length \\(a\\), the radius \\(R\\) of the circumscribed circle can be found using the formula:\n\\[\nR = \\frac{a \\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nGiven the side length \\(a = 6\\) units, we substitute \\(a\\) into the formula:\n\\[\nR = \\frac{6 \\sqrt{3}}{3} = 2 \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThe area \\(A\\) of a circle is given by the formula:\n\\[\nA = \\pi R^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the radius \\(R = 2 \\sqrt{3}\\) into the area formula, we get:\n\\[\nA = \\pi (2 \\sqrt{3})^2 = \\pi \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 = 12\\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12\\pi}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{12\\pi}."
