"To compute \\(\\tan 420^\\circ\\), we can use the fact that the tangent function has a period of \\(180^\\circ\\). This means that \\(\\tan(\\theta + 180^\\circ) = \\tan(\\theta)\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\(420^\\circ\\) modulo \\(180^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n420^\\circ - 180^\\circ \\times 2 = 420^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\tan 420^\\circ = \\tan 60^\\circ\\).\n\nNext, we need the value of \\(\\tan 60^\\circ\\). From trigonometric values, we know that:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 60^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(\\tan 420^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]"
