"To find the value of \\(\\log_{9} 4\\), we can use the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{a} b = \\frac{\\log_{c} b}{\\log_{c} a}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\log_{3} 6 \\approx 1.63\\). We need to find \\(\\log_{9} 4\\). Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{9} 4 = \\frac{\\log_{3} 4}{\\log_{3} 9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_{3} 9 = 2\\) (because \\(3^2 = 9\\)), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{9} 4 = \\frac{\\log_{3} 4}{2} = \\frac{\\log_{3} 6}{2} = \\frac{1.63}{2} \\approx 0.82\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\log_{9} 4\\) to two decimal places is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.82}\n\\]"
