"First, we need to determine the perimeter of the pentagon $ABCDE$. The perimeter is the sum of the lengths of its sides:\n\n\\[\nAB + BC + CD + DE + EA = 3 + 4 + 6 + 3 + 7 = 23\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find which side will touch the point $x=2009$ on the $x$-axis when the pentagon is rolled clockwise. To do this, we calculate the remainder when 2009 is divided by the perimeter of the pentagon:\n\n\\[\n2009 \\div 23 = 86 \\text{ remainder } 11\n\\]\n\nThe remainder is 11, which means that the pentagon has rolled 86 full times and 11 additional units. Since the pentagon is rolled clockwise, after 11 units, it will be at the position where it is 11 units past the starting point.\n\nThe side corresponding to 11 units past the starting point is $CD$. Therefore, the side that will touch the point $x=2009$ is:\n\n\\boxed{CD}"
