"To determine the number of different sandwiches that can be ordered at Alberto's restaurant, we need to calculate the number of ways to choose 2 different fillings out of the 9 available fillings. This is a combination problem, where the order of selection does not matter.\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\\[ \\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!} \\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, and \\( r \\) is the number of items to choose.\n\nIn this problem, \\( n = 9 \\) and \\( r = 2 \\). So, we have:\n\\[ \\binom{9}{2} = \\frac{9!}{2!(9-2)!} = \\frac{9!}{2! \\cdot 7!} = \\frac{9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 7!} = \\frac{9 \\times 8}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{72}{2} = 36 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different sandwiches that can be ordered is:\n\\[\\boxed{36} \\]"
"To determine how many cups of chocolate chips are needed to make 15 cookies, we can use a proportion based on the given information. We know that 8 cups of chocolate chips are required to make 12 cookies. Let's set up the proportion:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{8 \\text{ cups of chocolate chips}}{12 \\text{ cookies}} = \\frac{x \\text{ cups of chocolate chips}}{15 \\text{ cookies}}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we can cross-multiply:\n\n\\[\n8 \\times 15 = 12 \\times x\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\n\\[\n120 = 12x\n\\]\n\nNext, solve for \\( x \\) by dividing both sides by 12:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{120}{12}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\n\\[\nx = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, it will take 10 cups of chocolate chips to make 15 cookies. The final answer is \\boxed{10}.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $10$. I hope it is correct."
"First, we need to find the radius of each circle. The radius of a circle is half of its diameter.\n\nFor circle \\( A \\):\n\\[ r_A = \\frac{12}{2} = 6 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nFor circle \\( B \\):\n\\[ r_B = \\frac{22}{2} = 11 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe area of a circle is given by the formula \\( \\pi r^2 \\).\n\nThe area of circle \\( A \\) is:\n\\[ A_A = \\pi (6)^2 = 36\\pi \\]\n\nThe area of circle \\( B \\) is:\n\\[ A_B = \\pi (11)^2 = 121\\pi \\]\n\nThe ratio of the area of circle \\( A \\) to the area of circle \\( B \\) is:\n\\[ \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{A_A}{A_B} = \\frac{36\\pi}{121\\pi} = \\frac{36}{121} \\]\n\nThus, the ratio is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{36}{121}} \\]"
