"To find the length of \\( AC \\), we need to determine the distance between points \\( A \\) and \\( C \\). Given that \\( K \\) is the midpoint of \\( AC \\), we can use the midpoint formula to find the coordinates of \\( K \\).\n\nThe midpoint \\( K \\) of \\( AC \\) is given by:\n\\[\nK = \\left( \\frac{A_x + C_x}{2}, \\frac{A_y + C_y}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSince \\( K \\) is the midpoint of \\( AC \\), we have:\n\\[\nK = \\left( \\frac{A_x + C_x}{2}, \\frac{A_y + C_y}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\( OC = 32 \\) and \\( CB = 36 \\), we can use the distance formula to find \\( AC \\). Since \\( K \\) is the midpoint of \\( AC \\), we have:\n\\[\nAC = 2 \\times OC = 2 \\times 32 = 64\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( AC \\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\). \n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{64}\\)."
"First, we calculate the area of a full circle with radius 5 units. The area of a full circle is given by $\\pi r^2$, so the area of a full circle with radius 5 is $\\pi (5^2) = 25\\pi$ square units.\n\nNext, we calculate the area of one quarter-circle. The area of a quarter-circle is $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the area of a full circle, so the area of one quarter-circle is $\\frac{1}{4} \\times 25\\pi = 6.5\\pi$ square units.\n\nThen, we calculate the area of one semicircle. The area of a semicircle is half the area of a full circle, so the area of one semicircle is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times 25\\pi = 12\\pi$ square units.\n\nFinally, we add the areas of the two quarter-circles and the semicircle to find the total area of the region. The total area is $6.5\\pi + 6.5\\pi + 12\\pi = 25\\pi$ square units.\n\nThe area of the region is $\\boxed{25\\pi}$."
