"First, let's simplify the expression \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right)^4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a^4 + a^{2+1-3}\\).\n\n1. Simplify \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right)^4\\):\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right)^4 = \\frac{1}{a^4}\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply by \\(2 \\cdot a^4\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{a^4} \\cdot 2 \\cdot a^4 = 2\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify \\(a^{2+1-3}\\):\n   \\[\n   a^{2+1-3} = a^0 = 1\n   \\]\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n2 + 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(a = 42\\) into the simplified expression:\n\\[\n3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression when \\(a = 42\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
"To find the measure of angle \\( M \\), we need to understand the relationship between an angle and its complement. The complement of an angle is the angle that, when added to the original angle, equals 90 degrees.\n\nGiven:\n- The complement of angle \\( M \\) is 10 degrees.\n\nLet's denote the measure of angle \\( M \\) as \\( x \\).\n\nAccording to the definition of the complement:\n\\[ x + 10^\\circ = 90^\\circ \\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we need to isolate it on one side of the equation. We do this by subtracting 10 degrees from both sides:\n\\[ x = 90^\\circ - 10^\\circ \\]\n\\[ x = 80^\\circ \\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\( M \\) is \\(\\boxed{80^\\circ}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(80^\\circ\\). I hope it is correct."
