"To find the perimeter of the rhombus, we need to determine the length of one of its sides. The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles, so we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of one side.\n\nThe diagonals of the rhombus are 12 units and 16 units. Each half of these diagonals is 6 units and 8 units, respectively.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we can find the length of one side of the rhombus:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Side} = \\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100} = 10 \\text{ units}\n\\]\n\nSince all sides of a rhombus are equal, the perimeter of the rhombus is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 4 \\times \\text{Side} = 4 \\times 10 = 40 \\text{ units}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the rhombus is \\boxed{40} units. The final answer is 40 units."
