"To find the minimum positive number of days before planets X, Y, and Z are all in the exact same locations again, we need to determine the least common multiple (LCM) of their orbital periods. The orbital periods are 360 days, 450 days, and 540 days.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of these numbers:\n- \\(360 = 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 5\\)\n- \\(450 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(540 = 2^2 \\times 3^3 \\times 5\\)\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in these factorizations:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^3 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2 = 8 \\times 27 \\times 25 = 10800\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum positive number of days before all three planets are in the exact same locations relative to the sun is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10800}\n\\]"
