"We can use the tangent addition formula to simplify the left-hand side of the equation:\n\\[\\tan (x + 100^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan x + \\tan 100^\\circ}{1 - \\tan x \\tan 100^\\circ}.\\]\nSince $\\tan 100^\\circ = 1,$ we have\n\\[\\tan (x + 100^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan x + 1}{1 - \\tan x}.\\]\nSimilarly, we can use the tangent addition formula to simplify the right-hand side of the equation:\n\\[\\tan (x + 50^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan x + \\tan 50^\\circ}{1 - \\tan x \\tan 50^\\circ}.\\]\nSince $\\tan 50^\\circ = 1,$ we have\n\\[\\tan (x + 50^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan x + 1}{1 - \\tan x}.\\]\nTherefore, the equation is always true for any $x$.\nThe smallest positive value of $x$ is $\\boxed{50}$. I hope it is correct.\nThe answer is: 50"
"We can take the cross product of two vectors that lie in the plane to find the normal vector.  We can use the vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.$  Then the cross product is\n\n\\[\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -16 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 16 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nThe normal vector is then $\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix},$ and we can use the point $(0,-1,-1)$ to find the equation of the plane.  The equation of the plane is\n\n\\[-x - y + z = 0.\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is $\\boxed{x + y - z = 0}.$\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $x+y-z=0\\$."
"To find \\(\\arccos \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right)\\), we need to determine the angle \\(\\theta\\) such that \\(\\cos \\theta = -\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nThe cosine function is negative in the second and third quadrants. The reference angle for \\(\\cos \\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) radians.\n\nIn the second quadrant, the angle \\(\\theta\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nIn the third quadrant, the angle \\(\\theta\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\theta = 2\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the values of \\(\\arccos \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\text{ and } \\frac{5\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) and \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\)."
"We know that $\\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}$, so $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v} - \\mathbf{w}$. Taking the dot product of both sides with $\\mathbf{v}$, we get $(\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u}) \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} - \\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1 - \\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$. Since $\\mathbf{w}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are unit vectors, $\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1$. Therefore, $(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) \\cdot \\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) = (\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{u})(\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}) = (1 - \\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})(1) = 1 - \\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$. Since $\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ ranges from $-1$ to $1$, then the largest possible value is $\\boxed{1}$."
