"To find the range of the function \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) for \\( 0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function over this interval.\n\nFirst, note that \\( \\sin x \\) ranges from 0 to 1 as \\( x \\) varies from \\( 0^\\circ \\) to \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore, \\( \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\) ranges from 0 to 1.\n\nNext, consider the function \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\). As \\( \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\) ranges from 0 to 1, \\( \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) will range from \\( -\\infty \\) to \\( 0 \\). This is because the logarithm function \\( \\log_2 \\) is continuous and strictly increasing on its domain.\n\nThus, the range of the function \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) for \\( 0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Range:} \\boxed{(-\\infty, 0]} \\]"
"We can use the sum-to-product identities to simplify the expression. Notice that:\n\\[\n\\sin A + \\sin B = 2 \\sin \\left( \\frac{A+B}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-B}{2} \\right).\n\\]\n\nWe can pair the terms as follows:\n\\[\n\\left( \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12} \\right) + \\left( \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} \\right) + \\left( \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} \\right).\n\\]\n\nEach pair sums to:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\cos 0 = 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 = 2.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all three pairs is:\n\\[\n2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the given expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}.\n\\]"
