"First, we need to find the length of $AC$ using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{AB^2 + BC^2} = \\sqrt{10^2 + 24^2} = \\sqrt{100 + 576} = \\sqrt{676} = 26.\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the length of the altitude $BM$. The area of $\\triangle ABC$ can be calculated as:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AB \\times BC = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 24 = 120.\n\\]\n\nNow, $BM$ is the altitude of $\\triangle ABC$, so:\n\\[\nBM = \\frac{2 \\times \\text{Area}}{AC} = \\frac{2 \\times 120}{26} = \\frac{240}{26} = 10.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, $\\cos \\angle ABM = \\frac{BM}{AB} = \\frac{10}{20} = \\boxed{0.5}$."
"To find \\(\\tan \\theta\\) for a point with rectangular coordinates \\((10, -18, 35)\\) and spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we need to use the relationships between rectangular and spherical coordinates.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(x = 10\\)\n- \\(y = -18\\)\n- \\(z = 35\\)\n\nIn spherical coordinates, the relationships are:\n- \\(\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\\)\n- \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\)\n- \\(\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\\)\n\nWe need to find \\(\\tan \\theta\\). Using the given rectangular coordinates:\n\\[\n\\tan \\theta = \\frac{y}{x} = \\frac{-18}{10} = -\\frac{9}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan \\theta\\) is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{9}{5}}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(-\\frac{9}{5}\\). I hope it is correct."
"To find \\((\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^{-1}\\), we need to compute the inverse of the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). Recall that the inverse of a matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is the identity matrix \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) if \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is invertible. Therefore, \\((\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\\\ 13 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo determine if \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is invertible, we need to check if its determinant is non-zero. The determinant of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{A}) = (2)(-2) - (7)(13) = -4 - 91 = -95\n\\]\n\nSince the determinant is non-zero, \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is invertible. Therefore, \\((\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\\).\n\nThe identity matrix \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the 2x2 identity matrix. Thus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathbf{I}}\n\\]"
